This is a classic Spring Boot configuration error!
The root cause is right at the end of your stack trace: Not a managed type: class com.fincore.VoucherService.Models.BranchMaster.
This happens when Spring Data JPA tries to create a repository, but the EntityManager doesn't know that your @Entity class exists. If we look closely at your **OracleDbConfig.java** file, there is a copy-paste mismatch in the package scanning path.
Spring is looking for entities in JournalService instead of VoucherService.
### How to Fix It
Open your com.fincore.VoucherService.config.OracleDbConfig file and locate the oracleEntityManagerFactory bean method.
**Change this line:**
```java
.packages("com.fincore.JournalService.Models") // <--- Incorrect package path

```
**To this:**
```java
.packages("com.fincore.VoucherService.Models") // <--- Corrected package path

```
### The Corrected Method
Here is exactly how that method should look in your OracleDbConfig.java file:
```java
    @Primary
    @Bean(name = "oracleEntityManagerFactory")
    public LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean oracleEntityManagerFactory(
            EntityManagerFactoryBuilder builder,
            @Qualifier("oracleDataSource") DataSource dataSource) {

        log.info("ORACLE_EMF_INIT | Building EntityManagerFactory for com.fincore.VoucherService.Models");
        
        Map<String, Object> properties = new HashMap<>();
        properties.put("hibernate.dialect", "org.hibernate.dialect.OracleDialect");
        properties.put("hibernate.hbm2ddl.auto", "validate");

        return builder
                .dataSource(dataSource)
                .packages("com.fincore.VoucherService.Models") // FIX IS HERE
                .persistenceUnit("oracle")
                .properties(properties)
                .build();
    }

```
### Why this happened
When you define a custom EntityManagerFactory (which you did here to support multiple databases like Oracle and Hive), Spring Boot disables its automatic @EntityScan. You have to explicitly tell the builder which package contains your @Entity classes so Hibernate can map them. Once you point it to VoucherService.Models, it will find BranchMaster and successfully build the branchMasterRepository.
Save the file, rebuild your project, and restart the service!
